    "created_at": "2018-03-07T01:11:47Z",
    "updated_at": "2018-03-07T01:12:28Z",
    "body": "@domenic Isn't there a difference between a generic and a semantic container?\r\n\r\nGeneric container: div\r\nSemantic container: header, main, footer, nav, hgroup, section, article, fieldset\r\nWrong?\r\n\r\nThis reminds me of dl now taking div for groups instead of say dgroup or dg.\r\n\r\nIf - say - a screen reader looks just at the div it cannot know - without its surrounding (edit: inner as well as outer) context, which semantic meaning that div \"might\" have. If you then copy+paste parts of html documents, meaning can even get lost (because the wrapping context that gives divs their meaning is lost), right?\r\n\r\n* * *\r\n\r\nThe other thing: I will be honest, the real issue at hand here however is that... while you can wrap from controls into their labels or use for attributes and place them in arbitrary places into the dom, you then cannot use css selectors to walk from the input/select/textarea control to the label. Because of that all kinds of wrapping must take place, even in the times of flexbox/grid.\r\n\r\nMaybe `input:label` / `select:label` / `textarea:label` could be a way to select the corresponding label/s?",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
